---
import LegalPage from '../../layouts/LegalPage.astro';

/**
 * /plan/disclosure
 *
 * The page every affiliate link on the site points at. It has to answer three
 * questions a reader can reasonably ask: who pays for this, does it change what
 * gets written, and what happens if I click.
 *
 * Written as a policy page rather than a guide article on purpose. It carries no
 * photographs, and CLAUDE.md excludes the legal and utility set from the imagery
 * rule for exactly this reason: an illustrated commission disclosure is
 * decoration pretending to be content.
 *
 * Everything asserted here has to stay true of the actual build. If a paid
 * placement, a sponsored listing or a second partner ever ships, this page
 * changes in the same commit.
 */
const SECTIONS = [
  { id: 'short-version', label: 'The short version' },
  { id: 'who-we-work-with', label: 'Who we work with' },
  { id: 'what-a-link-does', label: 'What a link does' },
  { id: 'what-it-does-not-buy', label: 'What it does not buy' },
  { id: 'photographs-and-reviews', label: 'Photographs and reviews' },
  { id: 'ratings', label: 'Ratings and scores' },
  { id: 'telling-us', label: 'Telling us we are wrong' },
];
---

<LegalPage
  title="How this site is funded"
  seoTitle="Affiliate disclosure · Visit Moganshan"
  description="Visit Moganshan earns commission on some Trip.com bookings. What that pays for, what it does not buy, and how property pages are put together."
  standfirst="Some links on this site earn us a commission when a booking is made. This page says which ones, what they pay for, and what they do not buy."
  updated="2026-08-08"
  sections={SECTIONS}
>
  <h2 id="short-version">The short version</h2>
  <p>
    Visit Moganshan makes money in one way: commission on bookings made through links to Trip.com.
    You pay the same price you would pay going to Trip.com directly. Nobody pays us to appear on
    this site, nobody pays us to rank higher on it, and no property has ever been given copy
    approval over a page about itself.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-we-work-with">Who we work with</h2>
  <p>
    One booking partner, Trip.com. We are in their affiliate programme, which means a link from
    here carries an identifier that tells them the booking came from us. There is no second
    partner, no hotel that books through us directly, and no commercial arrangement with any
    property on the mountain.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where a property has no bookable listing with that partner, we say so and send you to the
    property instead. A page here is not a shop window, and a place we rate is not left out because
    it pays nobody.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-a-link-does">What a link does</h2>
  <p>
    Every commercial link on this site goes through a path beginning <code>/go/</code>, which
    redirects to the partner. The redirect exists so that a link can be counted, corrected or
    removed in one place instead of across a hundred pages. It sets no cookie of ours and it
    records nothing about you; what the partner does after that is covered by their own privacy
    policy, and by ours on the <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those links are marked <code>rel="sponsored"</code> in the page source, which is how you tell a
    search engine that a link is commercial. Marking them costs us search value and we mark them
    anyway, because the alternative is lying to a crawler about the same thing we are being honest
    with you about.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-does-not-buy">What it does not buy</h2>
  <p>
    It does not buy a place on the site, an order in a list, a better description, or the removal
    of a criticism. Every property page carries one caveat, deliberately, and that caveat is
    written before the booking link is added rather than after. If we cannot think of an honest
    caveat for a place, we do not know it well enough to write about it yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    We have not stayed at most of the properties bookable around this mountain, and we say so on
    each page rather than implying a visit we did not make.
  </p>

  <h2 id="photographs-and-reviews">Photographs and reviews</h2>
  <p>
    Photographs on property pages come from the property, from the partner's listing, or are
    representative illustrations produced for this site. Each image carries a credit saying which.
    We host every file ourselves rather than pulling it from someone else's server, which is partly
    a licensing matter and partly a practical one: hotlinked images fail from inside China, where a
    large share of readers are.
  </p>
  <p>
    Guest comments are short excerpts, quoted as written, with the reviewer's handle as the source
    shows it, the date, and a link back to the review. They are not our words, we do not edit them
    for tone, and we do not pick only the flattering ones. Descriptions are written here from what
    we can verify, never pasted from a listing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ratings">Ratings and scores</h2>
  <p>
    Where a page shows a review score, that score belongs to the platform it came from and is
    labelled as such. We do not publish a star rating of our own for a place we have not stayed at,
    and we do not republish someone else's aggregate score as structured data for search engines to
    treat as ours.
  </p>

  <h2 id="telling-us">Telling us we are wrong</h2>
  <p>
    If a fact on a property page is out of date, if a caveat is unfair, or if you own a property and
    something here misrepresents it, write to us through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
    Corrections are free and do not require a commercial relationship. So is the right to have a
    page about your property say what is actually true of it.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
